WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.311 --> 00:00:01.144
(soft music)

00:00:01.144 --> 00:00:02.370
- [Narrator] Demand for life-saving

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:05.370
COVID-19 antibody drugs is on the rise.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.880
Just four months ago,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.210
drug maker Regeneron reported an average

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:12.540
of less than 5,000 dose
deliveries per week.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.210
By September, the number
increased to over 150,000 doses.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.650
This is happening as
hospitalizations remain high.

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:24.110
In late August, over
100,000 COVID-19 patients

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.880
were hospitalized nationwide,

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.670
a daily average not seen
since last winter surge.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.550
This comes as the delta variant

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.090
sweeps through the country

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.790
and healthcare systems become overwhelmed.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.450
For those who are vaccinated,

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.570
severe illness and hospitalization

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.250
due to the coronavirus still remains rare,

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.260
but millions of adults
remain unvaccinated.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.820
And for high-risk patients who do get sick

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.460
doctors are increasingly turning

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.860
to antibody drugs to treat them.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.160
To understand why they've
become so critical

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:55.980
in the fight against COVID-19,

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.863
you need to understand how they work.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.250
When patients are infected
by the new coronavirus,

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.120
their immune systems
should produce antibodies.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.430
These immune proteins bind to the virus

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.790
and prevent it from spreading
throughout the body.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.340
Antibodies against COVID-19 do this

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.850
by blocking what is
known as a spike protein

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.440
on the virus's surface.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.390
This protein normally helps the virus

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.570
latch onto and infect cells,

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.790
But for some people, their immune system

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:25.850
doesn't produce enough
antibodies to fight off the virus

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.300
or it doesn't produce them fast enough.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.160
This is where antibody drugs,

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.520
like the ones that Regeneron

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.190
and Eli Lilly produce, come into play.

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:35.810
To manufacture these drugs,

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.280
researchers isolate
what are called B-cells

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.090
from a recovered patients blood.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.420
B-cells are a type of immune system cell

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.820
that makes antibodies.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.190
In the lab, the B-cells are then mixed

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.270
with spike proteins derived
from the coronavirus.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.460
When scientists identify the B-cells

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.380
that are best at latching
onto the spike proteins,

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.780
they isolate the genes inside the cells

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.130
that produce the most
effective antibodies.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.670
These genes are then placed inside cells

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:05.390
and are grown in bioreactors
to create new antibodies.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.860
And these highly specific antibodies

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.180
are concentrated into a liquid solution

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.100
and then injected into
sick COVID-19 patients.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.880
This gives the body a
temporary boost of antibodies

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:17.990
that help it fight off infection.

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:20.350
In clinical trials, Regeneron's drug,

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.170
which is a combination of
two types of antibodies

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:25.650
reduced the risk of
hospitalization or death

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:29.370
by 70% in people with
mild to moderate symptoms.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.070
This is a big reason
by treatment guidelines

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:33.720
from major health organizations

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.950
now recommend the use of
monoclonal antibody drugs

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:38.320
for certain patients.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.670
- So this is a major breakthrough

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.370
in the care of COVID-19 patients.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:47.400
We have expanded our tools

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.470
to treat patients with COVID-19.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.490
And this is our first recommendation

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.440
for a therapeutic for those patients

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.240
with mild, moderate disease.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.740
- [Narrator] The World Health Organization

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.810
also recommends the use of
Regeneron's antibody drug

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.510
for patients who are severely ill.

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:03.810
- Giving them this additional antibody

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.120
seems to show an effect.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:06.500
And what effect is that?

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.210
A reduction in mortality.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:10.920
- [Narrator] As many hospitals
continue to be overwhelmed,

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.434
demand for antibody drugs remains high.

00:03:13.434 --> 00:03:15.851
(soft music)

